insert into public.questions (id, test_id, prompt, options, position, passage) values
  ('5227e674-a50f-5642-9745-0fc448b0e902', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'As used in the text, what does the word “raise” most nearly mean?', '["Create", "Congratulate", "Strengthen", "Lift"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The following text is from Jhumpa Lahiri’s 2003 novel The Namesake. Gogol, a child, is at a beach in Massachusetts with his father.

He watches his father <u>raise</u> a kite within minutes into the wind, so high that Gogol must tip his head back in order to see, a rippling speck against the sky. The wind whips around their ears, turning their faces cold.

©2003 by Jhumpa Lahiri'),
  ('c2183491-cc0d-5159-babb-c809ea630348', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["forgetting", "rejection", "imitation", "opinion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Novelist Leon Forrest admired William Faulkner’s writing style. Forrest’s novel Divine Days contains a long passage in tribute to Faulkner that is a perfect _____ of Faulkner’s style: anyone familiar with Faulkner’s writing would see the resemblance.'),
  ('414699ef-cf58-58e6-937b-febe51a03f28', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["designate", "amplify", "assuage", "convey"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Stock Exchange of Thailand in Bangkok, Thailand, is a dedicated exchange for investing in companies operating in Thailand, ensuring those companies receive certain regulatory oversight. Knowing this can _____ potential investors’ worries about bureaucratic minutiae and thereby allow them to instead focus on identifying sound business opportunities.'),
  ('89ebd908-87b8-5b7b-8f75-942ba8cb3d59', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["magnify", "scrutinize", "discount", "denounce"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The tendency to group authors together into distinct literary movements often encourages literary scholars to _____ subtleties in an author’s style. Those studying the works of Amos Bronson Alcott, for instance, may inadvertently overlook nuances in his work by focusing only on the most obvious ways in which his style corresponds to transcendentalism.'),
  ('689c72d1-34fb-544a-ab2e-931247176bb5', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["originality", "publicity", "complexity", "practicality"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Though it does not guarantee a book’s commercial success, _______ can play a big role in that success —a well-executed marketing campaign helped to make Richard Bachman’s novel The Regulators the fifth-best-selling novel of 1996.'),
  ('165cc600-7b9a-505f-987e-be3e60439667', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?', '["To show that attitudes toward the petition within the tribal community change over time", "To demonstrate that most tribal members are enthusiastic about signing the petition", "To portray Louis Pipe stone’s strong commitment to collecting signatures for the petition", "To suggest that some tribal members refuse to sign the petition because they dislike Louis Pipe stone"]'::jsonb, 6, 'The following text is adapted from Louise Erdrich’s 2020 novel The Night Watchman. Louis Pipe stone is collecting signatures for a petition from fellow members of the Turtle Mountain Band of Chippewa on the tribe’s reservation in North Dakota.
Louis Pipe stone tended the petition like a garden. He kept it with him at all times. In town, his eyes sharpened when he noticed a tribal member who hadn’t yet signed. Wherever they were—at the gas pump, mercantile [general store], at Henry’s [Café], on the road, or outside the clinic and hospital—Louis cornered them. If they were waiting for a baby to be born, he''d have them sign. If they were laughing, if they were arguing. If they were taking a child home from school, they signed.

©2020 by Louise Erdrich'),
  ('a2606e6d-1136-52b1-93ee-c34a6e82f089', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?', '["It explains where the language discussed in the text is from.", "It provides English translations of the Nuxalk words discussed in the text.", "It contrasts the language discussed in the text with other languages.", "It provides a general definition of the phenomenon discussed in the text."]'::jsonb, 7, 'In Nuxalk, an Indigenous language from the Pacific Northwest region of what are now the United States and Canada, wats'' means “a dog,” whereas wawats''tii means “a small or cute dog.” <u>This phenomenon, in</u> <u>which an element of a root word is repeated, sometimes with modification, within another word that is</u> <u>related to the root word, is called reduplication.</u> In this case, the element “wa” in wats'' gets repeated in wawats''tii. There are many examples of this type of reduplication in Nuxalk.'),
  ('194c2f2a-6ea2-54a4-a04b-44ce27a779f0', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'According to the text, what did Sun and colleagues discover about surfactants?', '["They can be used to contain microplastics within certain areas of the ocean.", "They are mainly composed of water.", "They have a much stronger effect on wave activity than microplastics alone do.", "They are helpful for removing microplastics from the ocean."]'::jsonb, 8, 'Microplastics are tiny pieces of plastic waste. Areas of the ocean with higher concentrations of microplastic particles also have smaller and fewer waves. A study by Yukun Sun and colleagues found that the concentration of microplastic particles cannot be the only reason for this reduced wave activity because the concentration of particles that would have the observed effect is much higher than that found in these areas of the ocean. However, they found that surfactants, chemicals often used to manufacture plastics, are released into the water from microplastics and have a much stronger wave-reducing effect.'),
  ('7983a325-6ab4-5be2-9543-fde3dd61af98', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '["Continued research and development in artificial-leaf technology is needed before the devices can be widely used as an energy source.", "The recent increase in the commercial use of artificial leaves as an energy source has encouraged many scientists to research ways to improve the technology.", "Artificial leaves were developed to mimic the natural process of photosynthesis in plants in order to store energy for long-term commercial use.", "Artificial leaves split water molecules into oxygen and hydrogen gas using catalysts more efficiently than plants do using the process of photosynthesis."]'::jsonb, 9, 'Artificial leaves are a developing renewable energy technology that mimics the process of photosynthesis in plants. These devices are silicon-based solar cells coated in chemical catalysts that activate reactions that split water molecules into hydrogen and oxygen gas. The technology, while generating lots of interest, is not yet commercially viable as a large-scale energy source. To meet this challenge, scientists from many fields are researching ways to store, transport, and distribute the energy the devices produce while other scientists are working to improve the cost and efficiency of the devices.'),
  ('0cfb91eb-92d5-5bb9-9fc8-6afdc8a6a6da', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the comparison?', '["10,864 square miles", "2,533 square miles", "4,453 square miles", "2,188 square miles"]'::jsonb, 10, '**Total Areas of Five Tribal Nations around the United States**

| Tribal nation | Location | Area (square miles) |
| Tohono O''odham Nation | Arizona | 4,453 |
| Standing Rock Sioux Tribe | North and South Dakota | 3,662 |
| Hopi Tribe | Arizona | 2,533 |
| Yakama Nation | Washington | 2,188 |
| Choctaw Nation | Oklahoma | 10,864 |

A citizen of the Standing Rock Sioux Tribe is a student in a geography class. The student wants to compare the total area covered by his tribal nation in North and South Dakota with the total areas covered by certain tribal nations in other states. Looking at the table, he finds that the area of the Standing Rock Sioux Tribe is 3,662 square miles, while the area of the Yakama Nation in Washington is ___________'),
  ('9d37ef96-aa49-5f32-94ba-4c9e8f793954', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice best describes data from the table that support Potzelsberger and colleagues'' conclusion?', '["Italy reported 57 damaging fungus species and 42 damaging insect species, while both Poland and Finland reported more insect species than fungus species.", "Poland reported 105 damaging insect species, which is more than either Italy or Finland reported.", "Italy reported 57 damaging fungus species, which is more than either Poland or Finland reported.", "Italy and Finland reported 14 and 6 damaging insect species, respectively, which is far fewer than Poland reported."]'::jsonb, 11, '**Numbers of the 23 Non-native Tree Species Reported and the Insect and Fungus Threats to Them**

| Country | Trees | Fungi | Insects |
| Italy | 14 | 57 | 42 |
| Poland | 10 | 25 | 105 |
| Finland | 6 | 11 | 28 |

Elisabeth Potzelsberger and colleagues gathered data on 23 non-native tree species grown in Europe. They analyzed reports from Poland, Italy, and Finland about the number of these species grown in those countries as well as the numbers of insect and fungus species that damage those trees. The researchers concluded that Poland reported a greater number of damaging insect species than either of the other countries did.'),
  ('982d2bb1-b2ae-502f-9603-5860508ab0b4', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice best describes data in the table that support the city planner’s conclusion?', '["The prevention of soil erosion was ranked higher for the general public than was the provision of medicinal plants.", "The provision of medicinal plants was ranked higher for the general public than were the other four services.", "The provision of medicinal plants was ranked higher for stakeholders than it was for the general public.", "The enhancement of pollination was ranked lower for the general public than it was for project leaders."]'::jsonb, 12, '**Ranking of Environmental and Sociocultural Benefits of Urban Agriculture (scale of 1 to 25; 1 =** **highest)**

| Social or ecological service | Project leaders | Stakeholders | General public |
| provision of medicinal plants | 22 | 21 | 5 |
| enhancement of pollination | 1 | 7 | 12 |
| enhancement of carbon sequestration | 17 | 22 | 21 |
| preservation of cultural knowledge and heritage | 8 | 15 | 13 |
| prevention of soil erosion | 13 | 11 | 23 |

Esther Sanyé-Mengual, Kathrin Specht, and their team surveyed three groups of people in Bologna, Italy —leaders of urban agriculture projects, stakeholders in urban agriculture (e.g., food researchers and urban farming associations), and the general public—to compare their views about the extent to which urban agriculture contributes to 25 social or ecological services that the team identified. The researchers used these, with a ranking of 1 indicating to rank the services that a group perceives that urban agriculture benefits that service the most. Using only the rankings shown in the table, a city planner in Bologna who is promoting a new urban agriculture project concludes that advertisements aimed at the general public should emphasize the project’s benefit to the provision of medicinal plants.'),
  ('4bd29660-eefa-5af8-ab15-e082c5898177', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support Bura and colleagues'' claim?', '["None of the species that emitted sounds did so before the simulated attacks, and nearly all stopped emitting sounds within ten seconds after the attacks.", "In most cases, the sound that a caterpillar species produced during simulated attacks was not produced by other caterpillar species during simulated attacks.", "Caterpillar clicks were emitted in a frequency detectable by birds that prey on caterpillars, but caterpillar vocalizations were not.", "Each caterpillar species tended to produce one sound during simulated attacks, although individuals occasionally made a variety of other sounds during simulated attacks as well."]'::jsonb, 13, 'Veronica L. Bura, Akito Y. Kawahara, and Jayne E. Yack investigated the evolution and function of sound production in silk moth and hawk moth caterpillars. They found that during harmless simulated attacks on isolated caterpillars, 33% of the tested species produced sound, which ranged from clicks in Manduca pellenia to vocalizations in Sphecodina abbottii. Although some insects use sound to communicate with members of the same species, the researchers claim that the caterpillar sounds recorded in their study are directed primarily at predators.'),
  ('8e221ad0-e5d7-5ad4-916b-92f58f02082c', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["Chinese immigrants who emigrated to New York City many years ago are more likely to speak several Chinese languages than are more recent Chinese immigrants.", "There are geographic differences in primary language within China that are reflected in the settlement patterns of Chinese immigrants in New York City.", "Chinese immigrants regularly change their residences between Queens and Manhattan after they emigrate, rather than staying in one borough.", "Taken together, there are more Cantonese and Fuzhounese speakers among Chinese immigrants in New York City than there are Mandarin speakers."]'::jsonb, 14, 'Over 600 languages are spoken in New York City in addition to English—one can find Aromanian spoken in the neighborhood of Ridgewood, or Hindi in Gramercy Park. Many immigrants from north China, where Mandarin is the primary language, reside in the neighborhood of Flushing (part of New York City’s borough of Queens) while those who emigrated from south China, where many people speak Cantonese or Fuzhounese as a first language, tend to reside in Chinatown, in the borough of Manhattan. Among speakers of Chinese languages, those in Flushing tend to speak Mandarin as their primary language while those in Chinatown tend to speak Cantonese or Fuzhounese as their primary language. This is most likely because _______'),
  ('96dc485f-de51-5a29-8ab6-0b0090867a8f', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["to appear", "appears", "did appear", "had appeared"]'::jsonb, 15, 'Robert Morris’s image was printed on \$1,000 US banknotes in 1862, making the former US superintendent of finance one of many nonpresidents _____ on US currency.'),
  ('06317b21-a8a8-5a0d-ab9a-507c2684491b', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["Trail which is 4.36 miles long—", "Trail: which is 4.36 miles long,", "Trail, which is 4.36 miles long", "Trail, which is 4.36 miles long,"]'::jsonb, 16, 'Many experts, like lawyer and cycling advocate Ernesto Hernandez-Lopez, have proposed bike travel as one possible way to alleviate congestion on the busy roadways of Los Angeles County, California. Indeed, local bicycle paths like the San Francisquito _____ have become an increasingly popular means of travel for commuter and recreational trips alike.'),
  ('f2d4d948-e143-5527-a799-705938166a7d', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["spans", "spanning", "span", "spanned"]'::jsonb, 17, 'Wisconsin, _____ 1,372 feet in elevation, reaches its highest point at Timms Hill.'),
  ('16686224-27d5-5374-bd8e-8d4c718a88da', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["select;", "select:", "select,", "select"]'::jsonb, 18, 'Over the years, dozens of architectural and archaeological sites important to Hawaiian history and culture, such as the Wailua Complex of Heiaus and Menehune Fishpond, have been added to the Hawai''i Register of Historic Places, and each year, a review board of experts that includes sociologist Alton Okinaka and architect Katie Stephens gathers to _____ which sites to add next to the growing registry.'),
  ('1289bde8-1394-5c83-9264-ccdc38319314', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["that—", "that;", "that:", "that,"]'::jsonb, 19, 'While the greater adjutant can be found in places like the Koshi Tappu Wildlife Reserve in Nepal and Chhnuk Tru in Cambodia, more than 80 percent of this endangered stork species is found in Assam, India. There, wildlife biologist Dr. Purnima Devi Barman is on the front lines of conservation efforts _______ through community involvement and scientific study, aim to bring adjutants back from near-extinction.'),
  ('025d2baa-77e2-5333-9ae0-c114124e4865', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["was", "is", "has been", "are"]'::jsonb, 20, 'In knot theory (the mathematical study of curved, closed loops), knots are characterized by their crossing numbers—that is, the number of times the knotted thread crosses over itself. The trefoil knot and the figure-eight knot, each with a crossing number below five, _____ among the simplest possible knots with the fewest number of crossings.'),
  ('7c1d82fe-8c85-5362-bfdc-b6358fe2d0e0', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["dendrochronologist:", "dendrochronologist,", "dendrochronologist—", "dendrochronologist"]'::jsonb, 21, 'At 4,000 years old, the Ancient Yew, a common yew (Taxus baccata) located in the United Kingdom, is one of the oldest known trees in the world. With four millennia of climate data in its tree rings, a single tree like this, claims _____ Valerie Trouet, can tell the history of the world.'),
  ('dd713331-9cd4-5ab6-a658-78ff639b2bcb', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["consequently,", "in contrast,", "additionally,", "actually,"]'::jsonb, 22, 'Space scientist Luminata Ilinca Ignat is excited about the potential of the new James Webb Space Telescope (JWST). Unlike its predecessor, the Hubble Telescope, the JWST is primarily an infrared telescope. Most light from distant galaxies is infrared; _______ the JWST can provide Ilinca Ignat and her team much more information about these galaxies than the Hubble could.'),
  ('6ba4850e-a74e-53e3-8932-b67a9ff7f0c5', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["For example,", "In addition,", "Of course,", "As a result,"]'::jsonb, 23, 'During many historic New York City parades, including the 1912 ticker-tape parade for US Olympic champions, the ribbonlike swirls descending on the scene were paper spools from “tickers,” telegraph machines that were used to transmit stock prices. _____ the tickers had long since been retired by the time of the parade for the New York Giants in 2008. Then, shredded standard paper enriched the celebration.'),
  ('7714a18b-4aaa-5f1e-ac78-e1eed08512a7', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to emphasize a difference between the two birds?', '["Identifiable by its very long, black, curved bill and its bronzy back, the great-billed hermit is a large hummingbird found in Suriname.", "One way to distinguish the great-billed hermit from the amethyst woodstar is to look at their bills: the great-billed hermit’s is curved, whereas the amethyst woodstar’s is straight.", "The great-billed hermit and the amethyst woodstar are two of the thirty-five different hummingbird species found in Suriname.", "The amethyst woodstar is a small hummingbird identifiable by its green back and its short, black, straight bill."]'::jsonb, 24, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Birds of Northern South America is an identification guidebook by ornithologists Robin Restall, Clemencia Rodner, and Miguel Lentino.
It lists the thirty-five hummingbird species found in Suriname.
The great-billed hermit is a large hummingbird found in Suriname.
It is identifiable by its bronzy back and its very long, black, curved bill.
The amethyst woodstar is a small hummingbird found in Suriname.
It is identifiable by its green back and its short, black, straight bill.'),
  ('92b1f9ed-5222-5608-9759-93179dcc083c', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'The student wants to emphasize a difference between the French Republican and Gregorian calendars. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Used in France from 1793 to 1805, the French Republican calendar includes the months of Ventôse and Floréal (the sixth and eighth months of the year).", "The second and fourth months of the Gregorian calendar are February and April, respectively.", "The timelines for the months of the two calendars don’t match; for example, the sixth month of the French Republican calendar begins during the second month of the Gregorian calendar.", "The French Republican calendar, which replaced the Gregorian calendar in France from 1793 to 1805, has twelve months, including the months of Ventôse and Floréal."]'::jsonb, 25, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

The French Republican calendar replaced the Gregorian calendar in France from 1793 to 1805. Both calendars are divided into twelve months.
The timelines for the months don’t match.
The sixth month of the French Republican calendar, Ventôse, begins during February, the second month of the Gregorian calendar.
The eighth month of the French Republican calendar, Floréal, begins during April, the fourth month of the Gregorian calendar.'),
  ('30d31ece-1f12-5aa6-92b4-422027c87f39', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'The student wants to compare the hardness of the three minerals. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Based on their Mohs scale numbers, diamond (10) is harder than quartz (7), and quartz is harder than fluorite (4).", "A mineral with a Mohs number of 10, like diamond, is harder than one with a Mohs number of 7, like quartz.", "The Mohs scale of mineral hardness can be used to order quartz, fluorite, and diamond by their ability to scratch other minerals.", "Diamond can leave visible scratches on fluorite, which is why diamond has a higher number than fluorite on the Mohs scale of mineral hardness."]'::jsonb, 26, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

The Mohs scale of mineral hardness is a ten-point scale that orders minerals by hardness based on their ability to scratch other minerals.
Minerals with larger numbers are harder than minerals with smaller numbers and can leave visible scratches on them.
Minerals with smaller numbers are softer than minerals with larger numbers and cannot leave visible scratches on them.
The mineral fluorite has a Mohs scale number of 4.
The mineral quartz has a Mohs scale number of 7.
The mineral diamond has a Mohs scale number of 10.'),
  ('4d36cba7-c3b6-5321-9a28-a35096609e23', 'b2a94573-598d-50b7-9485-0ba3c0ba94a9', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to begin a narration of Mexía’s collection of Wulffia maculata?', '["It was on January 25, 1930, in the state of Minas Gerais, that Ynés Mexía added a new specimen to her growing collection of botanical samples: Wulffia maculata of the Asteraceae family.", "Ynés Mexía collected a sample of Wulffia maculata in January of 1930 and Senecio chapalensis in January of 1927.", "While both specimens collected by Ynés Mexía are members of the Asteraceae family, Wulffia maculata was collected in Minas Gerais, Brazil, and Senecio chapalensis was collected in Jalisco, Mexico.", "Thousands of botanical samples collected from across the Americas by Ynés Mexía can now be found in one place: online at the CV, Starr Virtual Herbarium."]'::jsonb, 27, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Ynés Mexía was a Mexican American botanist.
Between 1917 and 1938, she collected over 150,000 botanical samples throughout the Americas. She collected a sample of Wulffia maculata in Minas Gerais, Brazil, on January 25, 1930. She collected a sample of Senecio chapalensis in Jalisco, Mexico, on January 17, 1927. These specimens are part of the Asteraceae family.
They can now be viewed online at the CV, Starr Virtual Herbarium.')
on conflict (id) do update set test_id = excluded.test_id, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options,
  position = excluded.position, passage = excluded.passage;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct_index, correct_answers, explanation) values
  ('5227e674-a50f-5642-9745-0fc448b0e902', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** In the passage Gogol watches his father "raise a kite ... so high that Gogol must tip his head back in order to see," which clearly describes moving the kite upward into the sky. The context of height and looking up signals a physical upward motion, so "raise" most nearly means "lift."
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Create" would mean to make or form the kite; the sentence describes the kite being moved into the air, not being made.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Congratulate" is unrelated to the physical action described and does not fit the context of a kite rising into the wind.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Strengthen" implies increasing force or durability, which is not suggested by the image of a kite being taken higher into the sky.
**Faster approach:** Visualize the scene—if the sentence makes the narrator look up to see something higher, substitute "lift" to confirm meaning quickly.'),
  ('c2183491-cc0d-5159-babb-c809ea630348', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence says Forrest’s passage is a "perfect _____ of Faulkner’s style: anyone familiar with Faulkner’s writing would see the resemblance." The noun that best expresses a work that intentionally mirrors another author’s manner is "imitation," which directly accounts for producing a passage that resembles Faulkner’s style.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Forgetting" does not fit the context; it implies failing to remember, which contradicts the idea of resemblance.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Rejection" means refusal or dismissal and does not convey copying or resemblance to Faulkner’s style.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Opinion" refers to a belief or judgment and does not indicate producing a passage that resembles another writer’s style.
**Faster approach:** Use the surrounding clue "resemblance" to eliminate choices that don’t mean copying or mimicking; the word that means copying (imitation) is the correct fill.'),
  ('414699ef-cf58-58e6-937b-febe51a03f28', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The context requires a verb that means to lessen or relieve investors’ worries so they can focus on opportunities; "assuage" means to make an unpleasant feeling less intense, which fits both logically and precisely.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Designate" means to name or officially appoint; it does not mean to reduce or affect worries, so it fails to fit the intended causal relationship in the sentence.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Amplify" means to increase or make louder; using it would contradict the intended effect of reducing worries and therefore is the opposite of the required meaning.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Convey" means to communicate or transport; while it could relate to information, it does not mean to alleviate worries and thus does not complete the sentence appropriately.
**Faster approach:** Identify the intended effect on "worries" (reduce vs. increase vs. name/communicate); eliminate words with opposite or unrelated meanings, leaving the synonym for "relieve."'),
  ('89ebd908-87b8-5b7b-8f75-942ba8cb3d59', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence says that grouping authors into movements leads scholars to fail to notice subtle features; the next sentence makes this explicit by saying that scholars "may inadvertently overlook nuances." To "discount" subtleties means to regard them as unimportant or to dismiss them, which matches the idea of overlooking nuances.
**Choice A is incorrect.** To "magnify" subtleties means to make them seem larger or more important, which conflicts with the idea of overlooking nuances.
**Choice B is incorrect.** To "scrutinize" subtleties means to examine them closely, the opposite of overlooking or dismissing them.
**Choice D is incorrect.** To "denounce" subtleties would mean to publicly condemn them, which is not supported by the context that scholars simply fail to notice or give weight to subtle aspects.
**Faster approach:** Use the sentence that follows as a context clue—because it says scholars "may inadvertently overlook nuances," choose the option meaning "dismiss" or "ignore." For words-in-context questions, always match the surrounding explicit signals (like "overlook") to pick the word with the corresponding meaning.'),
  ('689c72d1-34fb-544a-ab2e-931247176bb5', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence cites a marketing campaign as the reason a book became a best seller, so a word meaning public exposure or promotion fits: "publicity" refers to the attention a book receives from marketing and media, and it logically can play a large role in commercial success without guaranteeing it.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Originality" refers to how novel or unique a book’s content is; while originality can affect sales, it doesn’t connect to the explicit example about a marketing campaign.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Complexity" describes the intricacy of a book’s content and is unrelated to marketing or the example given.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Practicality" concerns usefulness or practicality of content and does not explain how a marketing campaign could drive sales.
**Faster approach:** Match the blank to the example that follows; pick the option whose meaning directly aligns with the specific example (here, marketing → publicity).'),
  ('165cc600-7b9a-505f-987e-be3e60439667', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage focuses on Louis Pipe stone’s repeated actions—keeping the petition with him, sharpening his eyes to spot people, and cornering them in many locations—showing his persistent, determined effort to collect signatures. The details about where and when he gets people to sign (gas pump, mercantile, café, road, clinic; waiting for a baby; laughing; arguing; taking a child home) emphasize his strong commitment.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not describe changing attitudes over time; it only describes Louis’s consistent behavior in soliciting signatures.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The text does not claim that most tribal members are enthusiastic; it only says Louis gets people to sign in many situations, not that they are enthusiastic.
**Choice D is incorrect.** There is no suggestion that members refuse to sign or dislike Louis; the passage describes successful solicitation rather than refusal.
**Faster approach:** Identify the repeated actions and details about one character—those details usually indicate the passage’s main purpose (character portrayal) rather than a broader community trend or motive.'),
  ('a2606e6d-1136-52b1-93ee-c34a6e82f089', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The underlined sentence explicitly names and defines the phenomenon being discussed: "reduplication," described as "an element of a root word is repeated, sometimes with modification, within another word that is related to the root word." That sentence gives a general definition that applies to the examples that follow.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The sentence does not identify geographic origin; that information appears earlier in the passage ("Pacific Northwest region ...").
**Choice B is incorrect.** The English translations of the specific Nuxalk words are provided elsewhere in the passage ("wats'' means ''a dog'' ... wawats''tii means ''a small or cute dog''").
**Choice C is incorrect.** The sentence does not compare Nuxalk to other languages; it only defines a linguistic phenomenon within Nuxalk.
**Faster approach:** Scan for the verb "is called" or phrasing like "this phenomenon"—those signal a definition, so choose the option indicating a definition.'),
  ('194c2f2a-6ea2-54a4-a04b-44ce27a779f0', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage states that surfactants released from microplastics "have a much stronger wave-reducing effect," which directly supports that surfactants affect wave activity more strongly than microplastics alone.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not say surfactants contain or confine microplastics to areas of the ocean; it discusses their effect on waves, not on distribution of particles.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage describes surfactants as chemicals used to manufacture plastics and does not claim they are mainly composed of water.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage gives no information that surfactants help remove microplastics; instead it describes their impact on wave behavior.
**Faster approach:** Scan the sentence mentioning surfactants and read the clause describing their effect; the comparison phrase "much stronger wave-reducing effect" immediately identifies the correct answer.'),
  ('7983a325-6ab4-5be2-9543-fde3dd61af98', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage explains what artificial leaves are and notes that, although they generate interest, they "are not yet commercially viable as a large-scale energy source." It goes on to say that scientists are researching ways to store, transport, distribute energy and to improve cost and efficiency—clearly indicating that continued research and development is needed before wide use is possible.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage says artificial leaves are not yet commercially viable; it does not claim there has been a recent increase in commercial use that spurred research.
**Choice C is incorrect.** While the passage says artificial leaves mimic photosynthesis, it does not state they were developed explicitly to store energy for long-term commercial use.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage describes the devices splitting water with catalysts but does not compare their efficiency to plants or claim they are more efficient than photosynthesis.
**Faster approach:** Read the concluding sentence(s) for the author’s main point—they usually state the passage’s central idea about the technology’s current status and next steps.'),
  ('0cfb91eb-92d5-5bb9-9fc8-6afdc8a6a6da', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The table lists the Yakama Nation (Washington) with an area of $2,188$ square miles, so $2,188$ square miles most directly completes the sentence comparing the student’s tribal nation with the Yakama Nation.
**Choice A is incorrect.** $10,864$ square miles is the area listed for the Choctaw Nation (Oklahoma), not the Yakama Nation.
**Choice B is incorrect.** $2,533$ square miles is the area listed for the Hopi Tribe (Arizona), not the Yakama Nation.
**Choice C is incorrect.** $4,453$ square miles is the area listed for the Tohono O''odham Nation (Arizona), not the Yakama Nation.
**Faster approach:** Scan the table’s rows for the tribal nation name (Yakama Nation) and read the adjacent area value — direct lookup is fastest for table-based comparison questions.'),
  ('9d37ef96-aa49-5f32-94ba-4c9e8f793954', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The insects column in the table shows Italy 42, Poland 105, and Finland 28, so Poland’s 105 damaging insect species is greater than the numbers reported by either Italy or Finland.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although the statement about Italy (57 fungi and 42 insects) is accurate and Poland and Finland do report more insects than fungi, this choice does not directly state that Poland’s insect count exceeds both other countries’ insect counts as clearly as Choice B does.
**Choice C is incorrect.** While Italy did report 57 damaging fungus species (more than Poland’s 25 and Finland’s 11), that fact concerns fungi and does not support the conclusion about insects.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The numbers 14 and 6 are the counts of non-native tree species, not insect species, so this choice misstates the data.
**Faster approach:** Scan the insects column and compare the three numbers directly; the largest insect value identifies the country that reported the most insect species.'),
  ('982d2bb1-b2ae-502f-9603-5860508ab0b4', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** In the table the general public’s ranking for the provision of medicinal plants is 5, while their rankings for the other four listed services are 12 (enhancement of pollination), 21 (enhancement of carbon sequestration), 13 (preservation of cultural knowledge and heritage), and 23 (prevention of soil erosion). Because a smaller number indicates a higher perceived benefit, the provision of medicinal plants (5) is ranked higher by the general public than each of the other four services.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Prevention of soil erosion is ranked 23 for the general public, which is lower (worse) than the provision of medicinal plants (5), so it is not ranked higher.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Stakeholders gave the provision of medicinal plants a ranking of 21, which is lower than the general public’s ranking of 5, so stakeholders did not rank it higher.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Enhancement of pollination is ranked 1 by project leaders and 12 by the general public; pollination is ranked higher by project leaders than by the general public, not lower for the general public.
**Faster approach:** Scan the general-public column for the smallest number — the smallest value identifies the service they rank highest (best).'),
  ('4bd29660-eefa-5af8-ab15-e082c5898177', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** If sounds are emitted only during simulated attacks and stop shortly after attacks end, that temporal link indicates the sounds function as a response to predators rather than as general communication; the fact that they begin with attacks and cease soon after strongly supports the claim that the sounds are directed at predators.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Species-specific sounds show differentiation among species but do not establish that the sounds are aimed at predators rather than conspecifics.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Demonstrating detectability by some predators for some sounds but not others provides mixed evidence and does not directly show that most or all sounds are directed at predators.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The tendency to produce one sound during attacks describes consistency of signaling but does not connect the sounds specifically to predator-directed defense.
**Faster approach:** Look for the answer that links sound production directly in time or causally to predator attacks (i.e., starts with attack and ends after)—temporal association most directly supports a predator-directed function.'),
  ('8e221ad0-e5d7-5ad4-916b-92f58f02082c', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage explains that people from north China (where Mandarin is primary) tend to live in Flushing and people from south China (where Cantonese or Fuzhounese are primary) tend to live in Chinatown, and then notes that Flushing speakers tend to be Mandarin speakers while Chinatown speakers tend to be Cantonese or Fuzhounese speakers. The simplest explanation tying these statements together is that geographic differences in primary language within China are reflected in where immigrants settle in New York City.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not say that earlier immigrants are more likely to speak several Chinese languages; it focuses on origin (north vs. south), not length of residence or multilingualism.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Regular movement between boroughs would undermine, not explain, the observed clustering of language by neighborhood; the passage indicates stable settlement patterns, not frequent moves.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage describes which neighborhoods favor which languages but gives no information implying that Cantonese and Fuzhounese speakers outnumber Mandarin speakers citywide.
**Faster approach:** Look for an answer that links the stated cause (region of origin in China) to the effect (distribution of primary languages in NYC neighborhoods); choose the option that restates that causal relationship.'),
  ('96dc485f-de51-5a29-8ab6-0b0090867a8f', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The infinitive phrase "to appear" correctly completes the noun phrase "one of many nonpresidents to appear on US currency," which describes members of a group who have appeared on currency. The sentence already uses a past passive clause ("was printed... in 1862"), and the infinitive provides the intended generalizing modifier.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "appears" is simple present and would create a tense mismatch and an ungrammatical noun phrase: "one of many nonpresidents appears on..."
**Choice C is incorrect.** "did appear" is emphatic past and does not fit the reduced modifier slot after "one of many"; it would be awkward and ungrammatical there.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "had appeared" is past perfect and implies a prior action relative to another past reference, which is unnecessary and ungrammatical in this construction.
**Faster approach:** Recognize the common construction "one of many + to + base verb" (e.g., "one of many to win") and choose the infinitive.'),
  ('06317b21-a8a8-5a0d-ab9a-507c2684491b', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The phrase "which is 4.36 miles long" is a nonrestrictive (extra) clause adding information about the San Francisquito Trail, so it must be set off from the sentence by commas on both sides: "the San Francisquito Trail, which is 4.36 miles long, have become..." This punctuation correctly isolates the parenthetical information and preserves the sentence’s meaning and flow.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Using a dash after "long—" is ungrammatical here and fails to close a nonrestrictive clause; it also creates a fragment instead of integrating the clause into the sentence.
**Choice B is incorrect.** A colon before "which" is incorrect because colons introduce independent clauses or lists, not a nonrestrictive clause beginning with "which."
**Choice C is incorrect.** Although it begins the nonrestrictive clause correctly with a comma, it omits the closing comma after "long," leaving the clause unclosed and breaking the sentence’s punctuation.
**Faster approach:** Recognize "which" signaling a nonrestrictive clause—always set it off with commas on both sides; pick the option that supplies both commas.'),
  ('f2d4d948-e143-5527-a799-705938166a7d', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The phrase between commas is a nonrestrictive participial phrase describing Wisconsin’s range of elevation. The present participle "spanning" correctly forms a modifier that relates to the subject "Wisconsin": "Wisconsin, spanning 1,372 feet in elevation, reaches its highest point at Timms Hill."
**Choice A is incorrect.** "spans" is a finite verb; inserting it between commas would create an extra independent verb and break the sentence structure (two main verbs for one subject).
**Choice C is incorrect.** "span" (base form) cannot function here without an infinitive marker and does not form a participial modifier.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "spanned" (past participle) would imply a passive or completed action and does not appropriately serve as a present descriptive modifier of the subject.
**Faster approach:** For parenthetical modifiers set off by commas, use a participial form (present participle "- ing" for ongoing/descriptive sense) to attach the modifier to the subject; eliminate finite verbs that would create a second main clause.'),
  ('16686224-27d5-5374-bd8e-8d4c718a88da', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The verb phrase after "gathers to" should be the infinitive "select" with no intervening punctuation: "gathers to select which sites to add next to the growing registry." The infinitive phrase completes the verb "gathers" and should flow directly into the dependent clause "which sites to add next."
**Choice A is incorrect.** A semicolon ("select;") incorrectly separates parts of the same clause and would create a sentence fragment after the semicolon.
**Choice B is incorrect.** A colon ("select:") would signal a list or explanation to follow, but here the clause "which sites to add next to the growing registry" is not an independent explanation that requires a colon.
**Choice C is incorrect.** A comma ("select,") wrongly interrupts the infinitive construction and creates a comma splice or unnecessary pause; no punctuation belongs there.
**Faster approach:** When you see "to" + verb, read it as a single infinitive phrase — do not insert punctuation between "to" and its verb.'),
  ('1289bde8-1394-5c83-9264-ccdc38319314', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The relative clause beginning with "that" modifies "conservation efforts," and the parenthetical phrase "through community involvement and scientific study" should be set off by commas. Inserting a comma immediately after "that" yields "efforts that, through community involvement and scientific study, aim to bring...," which is grammatically correct and preserves the intended meaning.
**Choice A is incorrect.** A dash after "that—" would create an abrupt break inappropriate for a
nonparenthetical clause and would not correctly set off the parenthetical phrase.
**Choice B is incorrect.** A semicolon after "that;" is ungrammatical because a semicolon joins independent clauses, and "that" begins a dependent relative clause here.
**Choice C is incorrect.** A colon after "that:" is incorrect because a colon introduces a list, explanation, or quotation; it does not correctly enclose a parenthetical modifier following a relative pronoun.
**Faster approach:** Identify whether the phrase is parenthetical; if so, set it off with commas. If the clause beginning with "that" is followed by a parenthetical modifier, choose the comma option.'),
  ('025d2baa-77e2-5333-9ae0-c114124e4865', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The subject of the sentence is plural: "The trefoil knot and the figure-eight knot." A plural subject requires a plural verb, so "are among the simplest possible knots" is the grammatically correct form; the phrase "each with a crossing number below five" is a parenthetical modifier and does not change number agreement.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Was" is singular past tense and does not agree with the plural subject.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although "is" is present tense, it is singular and does not agree with the compound subject.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Has been" is singular present-perfect and also fails to agree with the plural subject; moreover, it changes the tense and aspect unnecessarily.
**Faster approach:** Identify the main subject (plural if joined by "and") and choose a verb that agrees in number—use a plural present-tense verb like "are" for compound subjects referring to multiple items.'),
  ('7c1d82fe-8c85-5362-bfdc-b6358fe2d0e0', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The phrase "claims dendrochronologist Valerie Trouet" is an interrupting attribution inserted into the sentence, so it should not contain internal punctuation between the verb "claims" and the noun phrase identifying the speaker. The clause is correctly set off by commas before and after the attribution (the comma after "Trouet" is already present), so nothing should appear in the blank.
**Choice A is incorrect.** A colon would be inappropriate because a colon introduces an explanation or list rather than marking a simple appositive attribution.
**Choice B is incorrect.** A comma immediately after "dendrochronologist" would separate the title from the name and create an incorrect break within the noun phrase "dendrochronologist Valerie Trouet."
**Choice C is incorrect.** An em dash would create a stronger break than the surrounding commas and would be inconsistent with the existing punctuation, making the sentence choppy and ungrammatical in context.
**Faster approach:** Recognize the clause as a simple attribution interrupting the main clause; such attributions have no internal punctuation between the verb (e.g., "says/claims") and the speaker’s name, so choose the option with no added punctuation.'),
  ('dd713331-9cd4-5ab6-a658-78ff639b2bcb', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence sets up a cause-and-effect relationship: because most light from distant galaxies is infrared, a telescope that observes infrared light will therefore yield more information. The transition "consequently" precisely signals that result, making the sentence logical and cohesive.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "In contrast" signals a difference or contradiction; nothing in the sentence is being contrasted with the statement about infrared light, so this choice disrupts the intended causal connection.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Additionally" would add information but does not express the cause-and-effect relationship needed between the two clauses.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Actually" introduces surprise or correction and does not indicate the logical result implied by the first clause.
**Faster approach:** For transition questions, identify whether the two clauses are related by cause/effect, contrast, or addition; then pick the transition that matches that relation (here: cause → "consequently").'),
  ('6ba4850e-a74e-53e3-8932-b67a9ff7f0c5', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage describes how earlier parades used paper from telegraph "tickers," then contrasts that history with the 2008 parade, when those machines were no longer in use. "Of course" signals an expected or unsurprising change over time and smoothly introduces the fact that the tickers had been retired, which explains why shredded standard paper was used instead.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "For example" would introduce an instance illustrating the previous point, but the sentence instead states a temporal fact about the retirement of tickers rather than giving an example of their use.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "In addition" would add another piece of similar information, but the sentence actually contrasts past practice with later retirement rather than merely adding a detail.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "As a result" would imply causation from the earlier statement to the retirement, which the passage does not support; the retirement is presented as a later development, not a consequence.
**Faster approach:** Determine the logical relation between sentences (contrast, example, addition, cause) and eliminate transitions that don’t match; choose the transition that signals the identified relation.'),
  ('7714a18b-4aaa-5f1e-ac78-e1eed08512a7', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** It directly contrasts a specific distinguishing feature from the notes: the great-billed hermit’s bill is curved, whereas the amethyst woodstar’s bill is straight, so looking at their bills is an effective way to tell them apart.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although accurate, it only describes the great-billed hermit and does not emphasize a difference between the two species.
**Choice C is incorrect.** This sentence notes that both species occur in Suriname but does not highlight any distinguishing trait between them.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It restates identifying details for the amethyst woodstar but, like A, fails to compare the two birds to emphasize a difference.
**Faster approach:** Look for answer choices that use explicit comparative language (for example, "whereas," "instead," or a direct contrast) to highlight differences between items.'),
  ('92b1f9ed-5222-5608-9759-93179dcc083c', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** It directly emphasizes a key difference by stating that the timelines don’t match and gives a concrete example: the sixth month of the French Republican calendar begins during the second month of the Gregorian calendar. This uses the student’s notes about mismatched month timelines and the specific months Ventôse and February to illustrate the difference.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although it names months from the French Republican calendar and gives their positions, it does not emphasize the difference between the two calendars or show how their month timelines differ.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It only gives basic information about Gregorian months (February and April) and does not compare the two calendars or highlight any difference.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It notes that the French Republican calendar replaced the Gregorian calendar and has twelve months, but it fails to emphasize the key difference in how the months line up between the calendars.
**Faster approach:** Look for the answer that both states a general difference (timelines don’t match) and immediately provides the specific example from the notes—answers combining a claim plus the concrete example are usually best for synthesis tasks.'),
  ('30d31ece-1f12-5aa6-92b4-422027c87f39', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The notes give Mohs numbers for the three minerals: fluorite (4), quartz (7), and diamond (10), and state that larger numbers indicate harder minerals that can scratch those with smaller numbers. Ordering by these numbers yields diamond (10) > quartz (7) > fluorite (4), which directly compares the hardness of all three.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It correctly compares diamond and quartz but omits fluorite, so it does not accomplish the student’s goal of comparing all three minerals.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Although true in general, this choice only says the scale can be used to order them; it does not actually provide the specific ordering of the three minerals.
**Choice D is incorrect.** While diamond can scratch fluorite and the statement is consistent with the notes, this choice offers only a two-mineral comparison and adds a causal phrasing (“which is why”) that is unnecessary; it does not compare all three minerals as requested.
**Faster approach:** Directly compare the Mohs numbers; list minerals from largest to smallest number to get the hardness ranking in one quick step.'),
  ('4d36cba7-c3b6-5321-9a28-a35096609e23', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** It uses the specific details from the notes (the exact date, January 25, 1930; the location, Minas Gerais, Brazil; the specimen name, Wulffia maculata; and its family, Asteraceae) to begin a focused narration of Mexía’s collection of that particular sample. The sentence presents the event plainly and chronologically, which is appropriate for opening a narrative about a single collection.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although factually accurate, it mentions two different specimens and dates, so it does not serve as a focused opening for a narration about the collection of Wulffia maculata alone.
**Choice C is incorrect.** This is comparative and expository—it summarizes relationships between the two specimens rather than beginning a narrative specifically about collecting Wulffia maculata.
**Choice D is incorrect.** This general statement about the entire collection and its online availability is too broad to function as a narrative opening about the single event of collecting Wulffia maculata.
**Faster approach:** For narration openings, choose the option that singles out the specific event with concrete details (date, place, and object) rather than a comparison or general summary.')
on conflict (question_id) do update set correct_index = excluded.correct_index, correct_answers = excluded.correct_answers,
  explanation = excluded.explanation;

-- PeaklyTest Jun 2024 · Reading & Writing · Module 2
insert into public.tests (id, title, section, description, duration_min, position, published, form_title, module_no)
values ('dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'PeaklyTest Jun 2024 · Reading & Writing · Module 2', 'Reading & Writing', 'Reading & Writing, Module 2 of the Jun 2024 PeaklyTest · 27 questions · 32 minutes', 32, 221, true, 'PeaklyTest Jun 2024 · Reading & Writing', 2)
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, section = excluded.section, description = excluded.description,
  duration_min = excluded.duration_min, position = excluded.position, form_title = excluded.form_title, module_no = excluded.module_no;
insert into public.questions (id, test_id, prompt, options, position, passage) values
  ('cff87aac-6b22-50dd-8628-11fabccb3965', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'As used in the text, what does the word "placed" most nearly mean?', '["Changed", "Arranged", "Enlisted", "Estimated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The following text is from Charles Chesnutt’s 1905 novel The Colonel’s Dream. Mr. French and Mr. Kirby work together.
Mr. French, the senior partner, who sat opposite Kirby, was an older man—a safe guess would have <u>placed</u> him somewhere in the debatable ground between forty and fifty; of a good height, as could be seen even from the seated figure, the upper part of which was held erect with the unconscious ease which one associates with military training.'),
  ('ee24a0ac-a97b-5fc5-9649-8b3f75cb7268', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["implement", "circumvent", "corroborate", "disseminate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The results of randomized clinical trials testing the efficacy of common medical interventions sometimes fail to _____ conclusions that practitioners reach based on their real-world observations of patients. While there are several possible reasons for this, one is that practitioners may overlook confounding variables that account for the results they attribute to the interventions in question.'),
  ('b30517dc-3b1b-5a4c-a627-71a6d1ad9004', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["enduring", "conspicuous", "uncertain", "deserving"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Sarah Marquis, who walked 16,000 kilometers across Asia, Siberia, and Australia, undoubtedly accomplished much, but her place in our historical memory is perhaps more ______ than that of a noteworthy “first” such as Anésia Pinheiro Machado, who was the first female pilot in Brazil to carry passengers and the first to make stunt flights, a deed for which she will always be remembered.'),
  ('05543c76-0592-5770-acd6-f286244adff2', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["monetizing", "precipitating", "stabilizing", "exploiting"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In the early 1990s, the price of mass-produced comic books rose dramatically, which had the counterintuitive effect of _____ demand: buyers who hadn’t previously wanted to purchase comics suddenly thronged the market, believing prices would continue to rise and the comics could be resold later at a profit.'),
  ('6e776694-f863-5814-bb55-a2dd5fdb4cfb', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?', '["To note a potential concern about the findings of a scientific study", "To explain how the behavior of a fish species has changed overtime", "To discuss an aspect of ocean acidification that is frequently overlooked", "To present a debate between two research teams about a cause of ocean acidification"]'::jsonb, 5, 'In 2015 Floriana Lai and colleagues published a study concluding that ocean acidification has a strong effect on the behavior of Gasterosteus aculeatus, a species of fish. However, Lai and colleagues'' study relied on a mean sample size of only 12 fish. In a 2022 review of various scientists'' conclusions about the impacts of ocean acidification on fish behavior, Jeff C. Clements and colleagues caution that relying on such a relatively small sample size can increase the potential for biased analysis. Such analysis, in turn, can contribute to reports of exaggerated effects.'),
  ('58845f1f-5905-5402-aea7-e533b9cf7895', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?', '["It suggests that CEP attendees held a diverse array of opinions about the Southern Christian Leadership Conference’s political philosophy.", "It illustrates the CEP organizers'' efforts to educate participants on a wide variety of topics.", "It establishes that criticism of the CEP was limited to a few individuals in the Southern Christian Leadership Conference.", "It underscores the extent of the CEP’s impact on the Civil Rights Movement of the 1960s."]'::jsonb, 6, 'Historians have argued that a crucial component of the Civil Rights Movement’s success in the 1960s was the Southern Christian Leadership Conference’s Citizen Education Program (CEP), which invited promising activists from across the South to its one-week training sessions in Dorchester, Georgia. Led by experienced organizers such as Dorothy Cotton and Septima Clark, CEP attendees—<u>more than 7,000</u> <u>in all</u>—participated in workshops on topics ranging from public speaking to legal doctrine before returning home and using their newly acquired knowledge to spearhead local civil rights initiatives.'),
  ('ae3823f8-cc40-5f31-92ef-b3d4075da363', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?', '["It introduces a natural phenomenon, refutes two potential explanations for that phenomenon, and then presents a third explanation for that phenomenon that the author regards as plausible.", "It describes an astronomical finding, discusses competing theories about that finding that the author regards as flawed, and then describes new evidence that supports an alternative theory.", "It discusses a physical process, evaluates possible causes of that process, and then states that a persuasive account of the process has yet to be put forward.", "It presents a scientific observation, describes a contrast between that observation and other observations, and then explains why those other observations should not be considered credible."]'::jsonb, 7, 'Asteroid 6478 Gault has experienced intermittent mass loss since at least 2013, but in contrast to some other asteroids with repeated mass-loss episodes, 6478 Gault has not lost mass at its perihelion (the closest point of its orbit to the Sun), and thus the loss is not attributable to solar energy-driven ice vaporization. And as Jane X. Luu et al. point out, the singular nature of impact ejection makes it untenable as an account of multiple loss episodes of similar duration over several years. Instead, Luu et al. are likely correct that 6478 Gault is shedding mass due to rotational instability.'),
  ('66bb96a0-8352-5e5b-a03a-b54d8f885950', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Based on the texts, how would Rubayet Bin Mostafiz and colleagues (Text 2) most likely respond to the information presented in Text 1?', '["They would recommend that engineers in northern and western Nebraska measure the moisture level in the soil beneath proposed structures before beginning construction.", "They would argue that engineers in northern and western Nebraska have misjudged the effectiveness of the techniques they use to reduce the effects of expansive soil.", "They would encourage engineers in northern and western Nebraska to use pile supports when building new structures.", "They would suggest that engineers in northern and western Nebraska consider the cost of replacing the pile supports on which existing structures rest."]'::jsonb, 8, '**Text 1**

In northern and western Nebraska, many buildings and other structures rest on soil that is expansive, meaning that it swells or shrinks as its moisture level changes. Civil engineer Jay X. Wang has noted that the effects of expansive soil appear slowly in the form of gradually growing cracks in foundations, walls, and pavements. Because these effects are incremental and can generally be repaired (though at some cost), structures in northern and western Nebraska are typically not built to resist them.

**Text 2**

In a 2021 study, Rubayet Bin Mostafiz and colleagues calculated that in the state of Louisiana alone, the annual cost of damage caused by expansive soils reaches nearly \$90 million. But they note that this damage can be mitigated: building structures on pile supports can significantly reduce the effects of soil swelling and shrinking.'),
  ('5b8e4506-ff9b-55d5-a896-b32dd65cb236', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which statement about Alpagali and other Africanist scholars is best supported by information in the text?', '["They likely view capitalism as having been more beneficial for Africa than historians of capitalism do.", "They likely have a different view about which activities should be considered capitalist in nature than historians of capitalism do.", "They likely make use of different types of evidence than historians of capitalism typically rely on.", "They likely differ from historians of capitalism in the methods they use to derive longitudinal economic data from archival records."]'::jsonb, 9, 'Thomas Piketty and other historians of capitalism rarely discuss domestic capitalism in Africa before the period of European colonization, implicitly presenting capitalism as external to and imposed on Africa. Crisalynne Alpagali and other Africanist scholars have shown, however, that in parts of Africa, export-oriented manufacturing, the existence of salaried labor, and other features of capitalism predated colonization. One reason for this discrepancy is that historians of capitalism tend to focus on longitudinal economic data drawn from archival records, which do not exist for much of precolonial Africa.'),
  ('4d2be23a-5174-5e9e-be84-1685010c09cb', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the student’s claim?', '["The majority of the most prevalent dog breeds have limited activity in the gene that regulates IGF-1 production.", "The gene that regulates IGF-1 production is not the only gene that influences body size in dogs but is the only such gene that shows within-breed version consistency.", "The degree of size variation among dogs is greater than that of any other land-based mammal.", "Some breeds with small body sizes and low IGF-1 concentrations have a different version of the gene regulating IGF-1 production than do toy poodles."]'::jsonb, 10, 'Geneticist Elaine Ostrander led an international collaboration in which Jocelyn Plassais and other researchers investigated the evolutionary history of size variation in modern dogs. The researchers identified multiple versions of the gene regulating the production of IGF-1 (insulin-like growth factor 1) in dogs and found that dogs of the same breed consistently share the same version of the gene. In a discussion of the study, a student hypothesizes that small breeds of dogs (for example, toy poodles) must share a version that represses IGF-1 production that would otherwise confer larger body size.'),
  ('323981f7-70d7-577d-b8e6-7f4cea8d50d7', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which quotation from a literary scholar would most directly support the claim in the underlined portion of the text?', '["\"While Baltasar and Blimunda alternates between realistic and antirealistic modes of representation, details suggesting the influence of Portuguese folklore nevertheless occur throughout the novel.\"", "\"Although Portuguese folklore clearly informs the style and occasionally antirealistic plot of Baltasar and Blimunda, the novel also shows the inarguable influence of the magical realist tradition of Latin America.\"", "\"The logic of the realistic plot of Baltasar and Blimunda is repeatedly and productively disrupted by the presence of imagery and situations drawn from Portuguese folklore.”", "\"Like many works in the Latin American magical realist tradition, Baltasar and Blimunda is indebted to antirealistic elements in the folkloric tradition of Portugal.\""]'::jsonb, 11, 'Scholars cite Pedro Páramo, the 1955 novel by Mexican author Juan Rulfo, as a foundational text of magical realism, the Latin American style of fiction in which antirealistic plot devices—often borrowed from the folkloric traditions of Indigenous and colonial societies in the Americas—are deployed in an otherwise realistic mode of representation typical of the modern novel. This style has exerted a decisive influence on authors around the world, including José Saramago, <u>whose 1982 novel Baltasar and</u> <u>Blimunda resembles classic magical realist novels in its juxtaposition of literary realism with folklore—</u> <u>namely, that of Portugal.</u>'),
  ('0821c06c-d576-57c3-b4d2-be3efcb5c7c2', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["analyze musical similarities in the songs of the Warlpiri and Karen (hta) Hin Lad Nai people.", "acknowledge that Indigenous songs are largely but not entirely composed using phrases taken from spoken language.", "incorporate the preservation of songs into their broader efforts to protect Indigenous languages.", "recognize that Indigenous languages likely have more unique expressions that represent ecological knowledge than represent other types of information."]'::jsonb, 12, 'Indigenous songs are an important resource for ecological information. Songs of the Warlpiri people in Yuendumu, Australia, convey information about wild edible seeds, and the songs of the Karen (hta) Hin Lad Nai people in Thailand offer detailed information about bees. There have been efforts made to preserve Indigenous languages over the years—e.g., the United Nations'' International Decade of Indigenous Languages (2022-2032). However, such attempts have typically focused on spoken language despite the fact that some expressions in these languages appear only in songs. Therefore, if those involved in such efforts want to ensure that a comprehensive range of information is secured, they must ______'),
  ('7e71f6aa-a69d-5dfd-abb2-5ea421dc8750', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["Few of the studies conducted in North America have been able to measure dissolved organic carbon and mercury levels with the same level of precision as in Fagnani, Guimara, and Fadini’s study.", "Fagnani, Guimara, and Fadini’s finding may differ from the findings of other studies due to a difference in environmental circumstances that affects the relationship between dissolved organic carbon and mercury in fresh water.", "Fagnani, Guimara, and Fadini’s study may have inadvertently measured a different characteristic of bodies of fresh water than their levels of dissolved organic carbon and mercury.", "The mercury levels reported in Fagnani, Guimara, and Fadini’s study were much higher than those reported in the study by Driscoll and colleagues even though the dissolved organic carbon levels reported in the two studies were approximately the same."]'::jsonb, 13, 'In a 2012 study that took place in Brazil, Enelton Fagnani, José Roberto Guimara, and Pedro Sérgio Tadini found a negative association between levels of dissolved organic carbon and mercury in bodies of fresh water. It may seem reasonable to be skeptical of this finding, since most other studies, such as research conducted in 1995 in the United States by Charles T. Driscoll and colleagues, have found that dissolved organic carbon and mercury levels rise together. Like the latter study, however, most studies of the topic have been conducted in North America, and many of those study sites have similar characteristics to one another, suggesting that _____'),
  ('c4932997-4dd7-5c40-9d63-9fc4fb880d9d', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["although Anisakis sp. and C. kanabus both require spotted ratfish as a host, C. kanabus was more adversely affected by warming-driven changes in the spotted ratfish population than Anisakis sp. was.", "warming-induced population changes among Puget Sound vertebrates may have suppressed some parasite populations, but there were no changes among the eight studied host species that affected two-host parasite abundance.", "population size and density of the eight fish species examined in the study were likely largely unaffected by rising sea temperatures.", "a parasite’s sensitivity to warming temperatures is negatively correlated with the number of unique vertebrate species it depends on throughout its life cycle."]'::jsonb, 14, 'As complex life cycle parasites, Anisakis sp. and Cucullanellus kanabus require multiple host species throughout their development. Extrapolating from parasite counts on spotted ratfish and seven other fish species collected from Puget Sound from 1880 to 2019, Chelsea Wood et al. found that the abundance of three-host parasites, such as Anisakis sp., negatively correlated with rising average annual sea temperatures; the abundance of two-host parasites, such as C. kanabus, was largely stable. Noting that fish and other marine vertebrates are especially vulnerable to climate change, Wood et al. observed that all three-host parasites in the study depend on at least two vertebrate species, while all two-host parasites depend on only one, suggesting that ___________'),
  ('001c6915-f1db-51e0-bc85-f5a069ae7656', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["the researchers'' theory is that moths mistake nighttime lights for the Sun, continually trying to reorient their bodies while flying near such lights.", "the researchers theorize that moths, mistaking nighttime lights for the Sun, continually try to reorient their bodies while flying near such lights.", "moths mistake nighttime lights for the Sun and continually try to reorient their bodies while flying near such lights, the researchers theorize.", "moths continually try to reorient their bodies while flying near nighttime lights, the researchers theorize, mistaking such lights for the Sun."]'::jsonb, 15, 'Entomologists Yash Sondhi and Samuel Fabian have tried to explain why moths fly erratically around light sources at night. Knowing that flying insects keep their backs pointed toward sunlight during the day, _______________'),
  ('dff9b88b-8127-5a41-b0e7-e16f5cd3a104', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["hemisphere and", "hemisphere", "hemisphere,", "hemisphere;"]'::jsonb, 16, 'In Finland, the Hankasalmi observatory site monitors activity in the upper atmosphere of the northern _____ in Antarctica, another observatory site, Zhongshan Station, monitors the sky of the southern hemisphere. Together, they are part of the Super Dual Auroral Radar Networkx2014;or SuperDARN, as space physicists like Kathryn McWilliams call it.'),
  ('c9a66d8c-13db-5186-b328-52b8b8805957', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["spectrum, while", "spectrum:", "spectrum", "spectrum while"]'::jsonb, 17, 'Butterfly is a 1988 painting by the Japanese artist Ay-O. Like many of Ay-O’s paintings, Butterfly, which portrays a swimmer performing the butterfly stroke, attempts to make use of the entire visual light _____ sporting rainbow-striped goggles, the rainbow-hued swimmer splashes through a wavy rainbow of water.'),
  ('cf013a80-3e03-5f8f-abc8-77e2cc17672b', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["groups. Established", "groups and established", "groups established", "groups, established"]'::jsonb, 18, 'The 20th century saw the founding of many Latino advocacy _____ to protect and advance the rights of Latino Americans, both the American GI Forum (1948) and the Mexican American Legal Defense and Education Fund (1968) contributed to the history of US civil rights.'),
  ('665824e8-601e-5554-98ca-bff02acb02d1', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["has pledged", "pledging", "pledges", "is pledging"]'::jsonb, 19, 'Latvia, Greece, and the other member nations of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization, or NATO, share a commitment to the principle of collective defense, each NATO member _____ to defend the others if necessary.'),
  ('e14051d3-7b02-50cf-a741-1b80d53eb465', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["nations: Costa Rica", "nations Costa Rica", "nations, Costa Rica", "nations; Costa Rica"]'::jsonb, 20, 'The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) tracks comparative price list data for its thirty-eight member countries. For instance, in July 2021, a hypothetical basket of goods priced at 100 US dollars (USD) in the United States would have cost 55 USD and 137 USD in fellow OECD _____ and Switzerland, respectively.'),
  ('ca4fe8c0-068e-57dd-b3df-038556715c90', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["For this reason,", "Granted,", "Likewise,", "In other words,"]'::jsonb, 21, 'With many elements, like bromine, scientists were able to isolate a relatively pure sample of the substance shortly after discovering its existence. _____ this wasn’t the case with all elements. The process of isolating europium, for example, took several years—five, to be precise.'),
  ('8224f3d6-44fa-5bcb-9742-829e83f4ffa9', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Granted,", "Similarly,", "Furthermore,", "Thus,"]'::jsonb, 22, 'Modernista architects championed nature in their designs.______ the wavy exterior wall and botanical window dressings of Torre Cortés, a Modernista private home designed by Salvador Valeri i Pupurull, couldn’t exactly grow in a forest. Still, one sees natural influences in Valeri i Pupurull’s penchant for curves (rather than right angles) and plant- and animal-inspired flourishes.'),
  ('4e3af691-aaef-5d90-8567-d732aa0a65d6', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["That is,", "In addition,", "By contrast,", "Next,"]'::jsonb, 23, 'In a 1993 study by Vales and Peek, the researchers determined the ratio of three different plant subtypes within the diet of elk: graminoids, forbs, and browse. _____ the researchers determined the relative quantities of the plants the animal consumed.'),
  ('d4cede88-6d1b-5e60-8d3d-167f950f5044', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to emphasize the size of the crater?', '["When a large object struck what is now Ukraine around 80 million years ago, it created Zeleny Gai, a massive crater measuring 3.5 km in diameter.", "Long ago, a crater now known as Zeleny Gai was created when a large object collided with Earth.", "Researchers have investigated the formation of Zeleny Gai, an ancient impact crater measuring 3.5 km in diameter located in Ukraine.", "Impact craters, like Zeleny Gai in Ukraine, are formed when a massive object collides with Earth, creating depressions in the land."]'::jsonb, 24, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Impact craters occur when a large object collides with Earth at high speeds.
Examples of such objects include comets and meteorites.
Zeleny Gai is an impact crater in Ukraine.
Researchers have determined that it was formed around 80 million years ago.
The crater has a diameter of approximately 3.5 km.'),
  ('eed0eb3a-cce7-5c34-8e42-4daae592152b', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'The student wants to identify the sculpture for which Arp won a Carnegie Prize gold medal in 1964. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["In 1964, an artist from Germany won a Carnegie Prize gold medal for a sculpture.", "The Carnegie Museum of Art awarded Arp a gold medal in 1964.", "Arp won a Carnegie Prize gold medal for the sculpture Sculpture classique.", "The Carnegie Prize is an international art competition administered by the Carnegie Museum of Art in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania."]'::jsonb, 25, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Artist Jean Arp of Germany won a Carnegie Prize gold medal in 1964.
The Carnegie Prize is an international art competition.
It is administered by the Carnegie Museum of Art in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.
Arp’s winning artwork was a sculpture entitled Sculpture classique.'),
  ('cbe681da-8d58-5ae7-ae04-16e5cd38a761', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'The student wants to provide a specific example of onomatopoeia. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["The term \"onomatopoeia,\" which refers to words that imitate the sound they represent, combines the Greek words for \"name\" and \"to make.\"", "The Russian word \"tuk-tuk\" both imitates and represents the sound of a heartbeat.", "Differences in how languages are structured can cause variations in the words used in different languages to represent the same sound.", "The languages of Korean and Russian use different words to represent the sound of a heartbeat."]'::jsonb, 26, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Onomatopoeias are words that imitate the sounds they represent.
Onomatopoeias used to represent the same sound often vary from language to language. This variation is due in part to differences in how languages are structured.
"Dugeun dugeun" is a Korean onomatopoeia that represents the sound of a heartbeat. "Tuk-tuk" is a Russian onomatopoeia that represents the sound of a heartbeat.
The term "onomatopoeia" combines the Greek words for "name" (onoma) and "to make" (poiein).'),
  ('0d7267e1-33ba-500d-9439-3d3e455e7a6b', 'dce2af40-e980-5056-b8ae-6da6f4dc9a4e', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to make and support a generalization about symbols on flags?', '["Many countries feature symbols on their flags, and the study of these designs is known as vexillology.", "Kazakhstan’s flag includes an eagle, a symbol that is important to that country’s national identity.", "Vexillology is the study of flags; accordingly, vexillologists are interested in flags from around the world.", "The flags of some countries include symbols of animals; Kazakhstan''s, for example, includes an eagle."]'::jsonb, 27, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Vexillology is the study of flags.
The flags of many countries include symbols like animals, plants, or landforms.
These symbols often represent an aspect of the region’s history, culture, or landscape. The flag of Kazakhstan includes an eagle.
The flag of Slovenia includes Triglav mountain.')
on conflict (id) do update set test_id = excluded.test_id, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options,
  position = excluded.position, passage = excluded.passage;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct_index, correct_answers, explanation) values
  ('cff87aac-6b22-50dd-8628-11fabccb3965', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** In the sentence "a safe guess would have placed him somewhere...," the narrator is describing an approximate judgment about the man’s age. Here "placed" means to set or determine someone’s position in terms of age, i.e., to estimate. The surrounding phrase "a safe guess" signals an inference, so "placed" = "estimated."
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Changed" would imply an alteration of the man’s age or position, which makes no sense with "a safe guess."
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Arranged" suggests organizing people or objects, not making an inference about age.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Enlisted" specifically means to sign up for military service; although military training is mentioned later, it does not fit the phrase "a safe guess would have placed him."
**Faster approach:** Substitute simple synonyms (here, "estimated") into the sentence; the one that preserves the sentence’s meaning is the correct choice.'),
  ('ee24a0ac-a97b-5fc5-9649-8b3f75cb7268', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence says that randomized trials sometimes fail to do something in relation to practitioners'' real-world conclusions; the intended meaning is that trials do not confirm those conclusions. "Corroborate" means to confirm or support, which fits precisely.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Implement" means to put into effect; trials do not "implement conclusions," so this meaning is incompatible with the sentence.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Circumvent" means to avoid or get around, which does not make sense here because trials are not avoiding practitioners'' conclusions.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Disseminate" means to spread or publish; trials failing to spread conclusions is not the logical relation intended.
**Faster approach:** Substitute each option into the sentence and choose the one that preserves the intended logical relation (trials confirm/support practitioners'' conclusions) — that word will be "corroborate."'),
  ('b30517dc-3b1b-5a4c-a627-71a6d1ad9004', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence contrasts Sarah Marquis’s significant accomplishments with the durable historical recognition earned by a clear “first” (Anésia Pinheiro Machado, who “will always be remembered”). The best completion should express that Marquis’s place in memory is less definite or stable than Machado’s; “uncertain” precisely conveys that contrast.
**Choice A is incorrect.** “Enduring” means long-lasting; that would contradict the clause saying Machado “will always be remembered,” which implies Machado’s memory is at least as enduring as Marquis’s.
**Choice B is incorrect.** “Conspicuous” means noticeable or obvious, which does not capture the intended contrast with inevitable remembrance and is not the most logical fit.
**Choice D is incorrect.** “Deserving” evaluates merit rather than the likelihood of being remembered; the sentence concerns relative certainty of historical memory, not deservingness.
**Faster approach:** Look for a word that contrasts with “will always be remembered” — choose the option meaning the opposite (uncertain) to match the contrastive structure.'),
  ('05543c76-0592-5770-acd6-f286244adff2', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The context describes a surprising increase in purchases after prices rose, so the blank requires a verb meaning "causing or bringing about demand." "Precipitating" means "bringing about suddenly," which precisely matches the sentence: higher prices precipitated demand from buyers who thought they could resell comics for a profit.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Monetizing" means converting something into money or a revenue stream; it doesn’t describe causing people to buy more, and it doesn’t fit the description of buyers thronging the market.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Stabilizing" means making demand steady; this contradicts the passage’s depiction of a sudden surge in purchases.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Exploiting" means taking unfair advantage of something; it doesn’t mean "causing" demand and therefore does not match the sentence’s causal meaning.
**Faster approach:** Identify the clause after the colon describing a sudden surge of buyers, then pick the option that means "causing a sudden increase."'),
  ('6e776694-f863-5814-bb55-a2dd5fdb4cfb', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage highlights a potential problem with the 2015 study’s conclusion by noting its small mean sample size (12 fish) and citing a 2022 review that warns small samples can produce biased analyses and exaggerated effects; the main purpose is to call attention to that concern about the study’s findings.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage does not describe behavioral changes over time; it summarizes a study’s conclusion and a later methodological critique, not a temporal account of changes in fish behavior.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Although the passage mentions an aspect (small sample size) that affects interpretations, it frames this as a concern about a specific study rather than discussing a frequently overlooked aspect of ocean acidification generally.
**Choice D is incorrect.** There is no debate between teams about causes of ocean acidification; both citations concern effects on fish behavior and the reliability of evidence, not differing views on causes.
**Faster approach:** Look for the sentence that states the purpose — here the second sentence expresses a warning about the earlier study’s reliability; that signals the passage’s main aim is to note a concern about the findings.'),
  ('58845f1f-5905-5402-aea7-e533b9cf7895', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The underlined phrase "more than 7,000 in all" quantifies the number of CEP attendees and therefore emphasizes the scope of the program; this supports the passage’s claim that the CEP was a crucial component of the Civil Rights Movement by showing its large reach and likely influence across the South.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The phrase gives a numerical total and does not indicate attendees’ opinions or ideological diversity.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although the sentence mentions workshops on many topics, the underlined phrase itself provides a total count and does not illustrate the variety of workshop content.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The phrase does not refer to criticism or limit it to a few individuals; it simply reports the number of participants.
**Faster approach:** For questions about an underlined phrase, identify whether it supplies quantity, description, cause, or evaluation—here it’s a quantity, so choose the answer about scale/extent.'),
  ('ae3823f8-cc40-5f31-92ef-b3d4075da363', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage opens by stating the phenomenon (intermittent mass loss of asteroid 6478 Gault), then rejects two possible explanations (not caused by perihelion-driven ice vaporization; impact ejection is untenable for repeated similar episodes), and finally presents a third explanation — rotational instability — as the plausible account. The sequence of introduce–refute–propose matches choice A precisely.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although the passage mentions competing theories and rejects them, it does not describe new evidence that supports the alternative; it simply notes that Luu et al. consider rotational instability likely.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage does not claim that a persuasive account has yet to be put forward; it states that rotational instability is likely the cause, so a plausible account is offered.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage contrasts this asteroid with others and rejects specific causal explanations, but it does not argue that the other observations themselves are not credible; it only explains why those mechanisms do not apply here.
**Faster approach:** Identify the article’s sequence ( phenomenon → rejected causes → proposed cause ). Match that explicit structure to the answer choice to eliminate distractors quickly.'),
  ('66bb96a0-8352-5e5b-a03a-b54d8f885950', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** Text 2 reports that building on pile supports "can significantly reduce the effects of soil swelling and shrinking," which is a direct mitigation for damage caused by expansive soils. Given Text 1 describes that structures in northern and western Nebraska are typically not built to resist expansive-soil effects, Mostafiz and colleagues would most likely encourage use of pile supports as a practical way to reduce future damage.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although measuring moisture could be useful, Text 2 emphasizes a specific mitigation —pile supports—rather than recommending soil-moisture measurement before construction.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Text 2 does not claim that Nebraska engineers have misjudged their techniques; it offers an alternative mitigation, not a critique of their current judgments.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Text 2 discusses using pile supports to prevent damage, not the costs of replacing pile supports on existing structures; that suggestion is not supported by the passage.
**Faster approach:** Locate the mitigation or recommendation in Text 2 and match it to the situation described in Text 1—choose the answer that repeats or applies the explicit recommended solution (here, pile supports).'),
  ('5b8e4506-ff9b-55d5-a896-b32dd65cb236', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage explains that Alpagali and other Africanist scholars have shown capitalism features in precolonial Africa and then notes that historians of capitalism "tend to focus on longitudinal economic data drawn from archival records, which do not exist for much of precolonial Africa." That contrast indicates Africanist scholars rely on different kinds of evidence than those archival longitudinal records.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage says nothing about whether Africanist scholars view capitalism as more or less beneficial; it only discusses what they have shown about its presence.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage contrasts types of evidence and historical focus, not disagreements about definitions of capitalist activities.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage indicates historians use archival longitudinal data and that such records are lacking for precolonial Africa; it does not say Africanist scholars derive longitudinal data from those records or use the same method.
**Faster approach:** Look for contrasts signaled by words like "however" and "one reason," then match that contrast to the choice that refers to different evidence rather than claims about value or definitions.'),
  ('4d2be23a-5174-5e9e-be84-1685010c09cb', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The student’s claim is that small breeds must share a version that represses IGF-1. Finding that some small breeds with low IGF-1 concentrations have a different version of the IGF-1–regulating gene than toy poodles directly contradicts the claim that there is a single required repressive version shared by small breeds; it shows small size can occur with different gene versions, so the claimed necessity is false.
**Choice A is incorrect.** If most prevalent breeds have limited activity in the gene, that might be consistent with small size being linked to reduced IGF-1 activity rather than disproving that small breeds share a repressive version specifically.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Stating that other genes influence size but only this gene shows within-breed consistency does not refute the student’s claim that small breeds must share a repressive version of this specific gene.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Greater overall size variation among dogs is unrelated to whether small breeds share a particular gene version and thus does not weaken the student’s specific claim.
**Faster approach:** Look for a direct counterexample — a documented small breed that lacks the proposed shared gene version; a single counterexample disproves a universal "must" claim.'),
  ('323981f7-70d7-577d-b8e6-7f4cea8d50d7', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The underlined claim says the novel "resembles classic magical realist novels in its juxtaposition of literary realism with folklore—namely, that of Portugal." Choice C directly states that the realistic plot is "repeatedly and productively disrupted by the presence of imagery and situations drawn from Portuguese folklore," which precisely describes a juxtaposition of realism and folklore and names Portuguese folklore as the source; the wording ties both elements together in the way the passage requires.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although A mentions both realistic and antirealistic modes and Portuguese folklore, it hedges with "nevertheless occur throughout," which weakly states presence rather than asserting the kind of active juxtaposition implied by the passage.
**Choice B is incorrect.** B emphasizes influence from both Portuguese folklore and Latin American magical realism, which introduces an additional claim (dual influences) not asserted in the underlined sentence and thus does not most directly support that specific claim.
**Choice D is incorrect.** D compares Baltasar and Blimunda to Latin American magical realist works and says it is "indebted to" Portuguese folkloric elements, but it frames the relationship as debt to antirealistic elements rather than describing the novel’s juxtaposition of literary realism with Portuguese folklore as the passage does.
**Faster approach:** For command-of-evidence questions asking which quotation supports a claim, scan each choice for (1) the exact elements named in the passage and (2) the same relationship between them (here, a juxtaposition/disruption of realism by Portuguese folklore). Eliminate choices that add extra claims or weaken the relationship.'),
  ('0821c06c-d576-57c3-b4d2-be3efcb5c7c2', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage says that some expressions in Indigenous languages “appear only in songs,” and that preservation efforts have “typically focused on spoken language.” To ensure a comprehensive range of information is secured, those efforts would therefore need to include songs in their preservation programs; incorporating song preservation directly addresses the problem the passage identifies.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Analyzing musical similarities between specific groups is not implied by the passage as necessary for securing the full range of linguistic information; the passage’s concern is preservation, not comparative musical analysis.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage does not assert that songs are largely composed of phrases from spoken language; it emphasizes that some expressions appear only in songs, which contradicts this choice.
**Choice D is incorrect.** While songs may contain unique ecological expressions, the passage does not claim they contain more unique ecological expressions than other types of information, so this overgeneralization is unsupported.
**Faster approach:** Focus on the problem stated (spoken-language-only preservation) and pick the option that directly remedies that problem (add songs), rather than options that introduce new, unsupported claims.'),
  ('7e71f6aa-a69d-5dfd-abb2-5ea421dc8750', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage notes that most prior studies were done in North America and that many study sites there share similar characteristics; this supports the inference that a study conducted in Brazil might show a different relationship because environmental circumstances differ, so the Brazilian study’s negative association could result from different local conditions affecting how dissolved organic carbon and mercury relate.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not discuss measurement precision or claim that North American studies were less precise, so this choice introduces an unsupported idea.
**Choice C is incorrect.** There is no suggestion that the Brazilian researchers measured the wrong characteristic; the passage focuses on differences among study sites, not researcher error.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage gives no data comparing absolute mercury or dissolved organic carbon levels between the studies, so this specific numerical claim is unsupported.
**Faster approach:** For inference questions about differing study results, pick the answer that attributes the discrepancy to differences in study conditions or context and eliminate choices that introduce unsupported specifics or researcher error.'),
  ('c4932997-4dd7-5c40-9d63-9fc4fb880d9d', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage reports that three-host parasites (which depend on at least two vertebrate species) declined as average annual sea temperatures rose, while two-host parasites (which depend on only one vertebrate species) remained largely stable. The most straightforward inference is that warming-driven changes in vertebrate populations suppressed some parasite populations (those needing multiple vertebrate hosts) but did not produce changes among the eight studied host species that affected two-host parasite abundance.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It asserts that the two-host parasite was more adversely affected, but the passage says two-host parasites were largely stable while three-host parasites declined.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It claims fish population size and density were largely unaffected by warming, conflicting with the passage’s emphasis that marine vertebrates are especially vulnerable to climate change and the observed parasite declines.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It asserts a negative correlation between sensitivity and number of vertebrate species; but the data imply greater sensitivity for parasites that depend on more vertebrate species (a positive—not negative—relationship between number of host species and sensitivity).
**Faster approach:** Link the observed pattern (which parasite group declined vs. which was stable) to the stated difference in host reliance; eliminate choices that contradict that direct relationship or reverse its direction.'),
  ('001c6915-f1db-51e0-bc85-f5a069ae7656', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The introductory participial phrase "Knowing that flying insects keep their backs pointed toward sunlight during the day," must modify the subject of the main clause that immediately follows. Choice B places the researchers as that subject: "the researchers theorize that moths, mistaking nighttime lights for the Sun, continually try to reorient their bodies while flying near such lights." This keeps the modifier attached to the people who possess the knowledge and gives a clear, grammatical sentence.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The subject after the comma is "the researchers'' theory," not the researchers themselves, so the participial phrase improperly modifies an abstract noun rather than the people who have the knowledge.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The sentence ends with a tag "the researchers theorize," creating a misplaced and awkward modifier and a disrupted sentence structure (it reads like a dangling attribution).
**Choice D is incorrect.** The phrase "mistaking such lights for the Sun" appears to modify "the researchers theorize," which illogically attributes the moths'' mistake to the researchers rather than to the moths.
**Faster approach:** Immediately check which choice makes the participial opener modify the correct noun (the researchers); eliminate any option where the opener would dangle or attach to an abstract noun or to the wrong agent.'),
  ('dff9b88b-8127-5a41-b0e7-e16f5cd3a104', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence as written contains two related independent clauses: "In Finland, the Hankasalmi observatory site monitors activity in the upper atmosphere of the northern hemisphere" and "in Antarctica, another observatory site, Zhongshan Station, monitors the sky of the southern hemisphere." A semicolon properly links these two independent clauses when the second begins with a transitional prepositional phrase ("in Antarctica").
**Choice A is incorrect.** Inserting "and" would create an awkward construction because the sentence would read "northern hemisphere and in Antarctica, another observatory...," which is redundant and less clear than using a semicolon or starting a new sentence.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Leaving no punctuation produces a run-on or fused sentence that fails to separate the two independent clauses.
**Choice C is incorrect.** A comma here would create a comma splice by improperly joining two independent clauses without an appropriate coordinating conjunction.
**Faster approach:** When two related independent clauses appear back-to-back and the second begins with a location or transitional phrase, use a semicolon to join them cleanly.'),
  ('c9a66d8c-13db-5186-b328-52b8b8805957', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The colon correctly links the clause about making use of the entire visual light spectrum to the explanatory clause that follows. The clause after the colon, "sporting rainbow-striped goggles, the rainbow-hued swimmer splashes through a wavy rainbow of water," describes an example of that use, and the colon signals that what follows illustrates or explains the preceding statement.
**Choice A is incorrect.** A comma plus "while" produces a participial phrase that appears to modify "spectrum" (a logical mismatch), creating a dangling modifier and disrupting clarity.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Leaving no punctuation yields the awkward sequence "spectrum sporting," which is ungrammatical because "spectrum" cannot "sport" goggles.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "spectrum while" without appropriate punctuation still produces a dangling modifier and fails to show that the following clause exemplifies the preceding claim.
**Faster approach:** Look for punctuation that signals explanation or illustration; when the second clause exemplifies the first, a colon is the reliable choice.'),
  ('cf013a80-3e03-5f8f-abc8-77e2cc17672b', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence requires a period to end the first independent clause and begin a new independent clause. "The 20th century saw the founding of many Latino advocacy groups." is a complete sentence. The following sentence, "Established to protect and advance the rights of Latino Americans, both the American GI Forum (1948) and the Mexican American Legal Defense and Education Fund (1968) contributed to the history of US civil rights." is then grammatical with "Established" beginning the participial phrase that modifies the organizations named. The capitalized "Established" correctly begins the new sentence.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "groups and established" attempts to join two clauses with "and" but leaves "established" without a clear subject or connection; it creates a sentence fragment because "established to protect..." would not have a proper subject.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "groups established" creates a noun phrase (groups established to protect...) but without punctuation or a verb between it and the rest of the sentence it produces a run-on or unclear structure; the passage needs a sentence break before the participial phrase.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "groups, established" with a comma creates a misplaced modifier and a comma splice: it links two independent elements improperly and leaves "established" as a reduced clause that should attach to a noun directly; the structure is awkward and ungrammatical here.
**Faster approach:** When a sentence contains two independent clauses and the second begins with a past-participial word (like "Established"), put a period and capitalize the participle to form two clear sentences; avoid trying to join them with commas or conjunctions that leave the participle without a subject.'),
  ('665824e8-601e-5554-98ca-bff02acb02d1', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The present-participial form pledging turns the words after the comma into a participial phrase (each NATO member pledging to defend the others if necessary) that modifies the main clause. That use is grammatically correct here and avoids a comma splice; it also conveys the ongoing, mutual nature of the obligation described by "share a commitment."
**Choice A is incorrect.** Has pledged is a finite (present-perfect) verb and would create a comma splice with the preceding independent clause; it also emphasizes a completed past act rather than an ongoing commitment.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Pledges is a simple-present finite verb; with the existing comma it would produce a comma splice rather than the intended modifying phrase.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Is pledging is progressive and finite, which both creates a comma splice here and awkwardly implies a temporary action rather than a standing obligation.
**Faster approach:** When a comma precedes a clause that would be independent, pick a non-finite ("-ing") form to make a participial/modifying phrase and avoid a comma splice.'),
  ('e14051d3-7b02-50cf-a741-1b80d53eb465', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence uses a plural noun ("fellow OECD _____ and Switzerland") and needs a noun phrase naming other members; simply placing "Costa Rica" after "nations" without punctuation correctly makes "nations Costa Rica and Switzerland" an appositive-style list of two examples within the same noun phrase. In other words, "fellow OECD nations Costa Rica and Switzerland" reads as "the fellow OECD nations (Costa Rica and Switzerland)", which is grammatical and clear.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The colon introduces an explanation or list that is separate from the main sentence; "nations: Costa Rica and Switzerland" would create an abrupt break and is not the conventional way to present two examples integrated into the noun phrase.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The comma after "nations" would set off "Costa Rica" as a nonrestrictive appositive, implying Costa Rica is extra information rather than part of the set "fellow OECD nations"; with "and Switzerland" following, the punctuation creates an awkward and ungrammatical structure.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The semicolon improperly joins two independent clauses; "nations; Costa Rica and Switzerland" is incorrect because "Costa Rica and Switzerland" is not an independent clause and a semicolon cannot separate a noun from its examples.
**Faster approach:** Read the noun phrase around the blank aloud inserting each punctuation option; choose the form that keeps the examples inside the noun phrase without creating a clause break—no colon, comma, or semicolon is needed between the plural noun and the names that follow.'),
  ('ca4fe8c0-068e-57dd-b3df-038556715c90', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage first says that many elements were isolated soon after discovery and then introduces an exception: not all elements were isolated quickly. "Granted," is a concessive transition that signals an exception or qualification, so it logically connects the general statement to the contrasting example about europium taking five years to isolate.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "For this reason," indicates cause or consequence and would illogically claim that the preceding statement caused the next; the sentence instead presents a contrast, not a cause.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Likewise," means "similarly," which would indicate the following idea is like the previous one; here the passage is giving a counterexample, not a similar case.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "In other words," signals a restatement or clarification of the same idea; the second sentence provides a contrasting exception with a concrete example, not a rephrasing of the first sentence.
**Faster approach:** Determine whether the second sentence contrasts, restates, or extends the first; then choose the transition that matches that relationship (contrast → "granted/however/ nevertheless", restatement → "in other words", similarity → "likewise", cause → "for this reason").'),
  ('8224f3d6-44fa-5bcb-9742-829e83f4ffa9', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage makes a concession: although Modernista architects championed nature, specific architectural features ‘‘couldn’t exactly grow in a forest.’’ "Granted," signals that the author is acknowledging a limitation while maintaining the overall point that natural influences appear in the design, so it fits the logical relation between the sentences.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Similarly" would claim a likeness between two items, but the second sentence is not offering a similar example; it is making a qualifying concession.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Furthermore" would add support or an additional point, but the sentence instead tempers the initial claim, so an additive transition is inappropriate.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Thus" indicates a consequence, but the sentence states a limitation rather than a logical result, so a causal transition is not suitable.
**Faster approach:** Look for the logical relation signaled by the next clause (contrast/concession, addition, similarity, or result); match the transition word to that relation—here the phrase "couldn’t exactly" signals a concession, so pick "Granted."'),
  ('4e3af691-aaef-5d90-8567-d732aa0a65d6', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The clause that follows simply restates or clarifies the preceding information—explaining that the researchers determined the relative amounts of the plants—so a clarifying transition like "That is," is appropriate. The phrase signals a rephrasing of the previous clause about ratios into more accessible terms.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "In addition," would introduce new information rather than restating or clarifying the same idea; the second clause does not add a separate point.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "By contrast," indicates a comparison showing difference, but the second clause does not contrast with the first—it restates it.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Next," implies a sequence of steps; the sentence is not presenting a chronological sequence but a clarification.
**Faster approach:** For transitions, identify whether the second clause clarifies, adds, contrasts, or sequences the first; then pick the matching transition word (clarifier → "That is").'),
  ('d4cede88-6d1b-5e60-8d3d-167f950f5044', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** It combines the factual details from the notes (the impact about 80 million years ago in what is now Ukraine) and explicitly emphasizes the crater’s size by calling it "massive" and giving the specific measurement of "3.5 km in diameter," directly using the provided numeric detail to highlight scale.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although accurate, it is vague and omits the specific size and age, so it does not emphasize the crater’s size.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It does include the measurement but presents the fact in a neutral, informational tone without language that emphasizes the crater’s size as strongly as Choice A does.
**Choice D is incorrect.** This choice gives a general definition of impact craters and mentions Zeleny Gai, but it fails to provide the specific measurement or wording that emphasizes the crater’s size.
**Faster approach:** For questions asking which choice emphasizes size, immediately scan for the answer that includes the specific numeric measurement and an emphatic descriptor (words like "massive" or "huge"); that combination is the best signal of emphasis.'),
  ('eed0eb3a-cce7-5c34-8e42-4daae592152b', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The notes explicitly state that "Arp’s winning artwork was a sculpture entitled Sculpture classique." To identify the sculpture for which Arp won the 1964 Carnegie Prize gold medal, the student should use that direct piece of information.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although true, this choice is vague and does not name the sculpture; it merely restates that a German artist won for a sculpture in 1964.
**Choice B is incorrect.** This choice notes that Arp received a gold medal from the Carnegie Museum of Art but does not identify the sculpture’s title.
**Choice D is incorrect.** This choice describes the prize and its administration, which is background information and does not identify the specific sculpture.
**Faster approach:** Always choose the answer that directly names or quotes the specific detail asked for (here, the sculpture’s title) rather than background or paraphrase.'),
  ('cbe681da-8d58-5ae7-ae04-16e5cd38a761', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** It gives a specific onomatopoeic word from the notes—"tuk-tuk"—and explicitly identifies it as a Russian onomatopoeia that represents the sound of a heartbeat, which directly fulfills the student’s goal of providing a specific example.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It defines the term and gives etymology, but it does not provide a concrete example of an onomatopoeic word.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It explains why variation occurs between languages, but it is a general explanation rather than a specific example of an onomatopoeia.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It notes that Korean and Russian use different words for a heartbeat sound, which is comparative but less specifically illustrative than naming an actual word like "tuk-tuk."
**Faster approach:** For a question asking for a specific example, pick the answer that names an actual word or phrase from the passage rather than definitions or general statements.'),
  ('0d7267e1-33ba-500d-9439-3d3e455e7a6b', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage states that the flags of many countries include symbols like animals, plants, or landforms and gives Kazakhstan’s flag (an eagle) as a specific example. Choice D makes the supported generalization that some countries'' flags include animal symbols and cites Kazakhstan as an example directly drawn from the passage.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although it is true the passage names vexillology and notes that many flags have symbols, A mainly restates facts and does not use a concrete example to support a generalization about symbols.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage says symbols often represent aspects of history, culture, or landscape but does not say the eagle on Kazakhstan’s flag specifically represents national identity; B overstates the passage.
**Choice C is incorrect.** This choice concerns vexillologists'' interests rather than making or supporting a generalization about symbols on flags, so it is off-topic.
**Faster approach:** Choose the answer that makes a broad claim about symbols and is directly supported by a specific line or example in the passage; eliminate options that add unsupported details or shift focus.')
